  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 114 AM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe open wave should be located in the vicinity of the Four Corners \non Monday, with southwest flow continuing to draw more Pacific \nmoisture into New Mexico. Showers and a few storms will spread \neastward into central NM Monday, persisting through Tuesday. \nGuidance is in good agreement that a deep trough will dig southward \noff the coast of California Tuesday, with a broad area of south to \nsouthwest flow prevailing over much of the Intermountain West. This \nwill push deeper pacific moisture into the southwest, with PWATs \nrising well over 200% of normal by Wednesday. \n\nAll things considered, model agreement with regard to the \ndepth/speed of this approaching trough is quite good through \nWednesday, with increasing uncertainty late week as it ejects \ninland into the desert southwest. The GEFS ensemble mean favors a \nmore progressive, positively tilted trough, while the GEPS and ENS \nmeans show a deeper, slower-moving trough. The latter solutions \nwould be wetter for New Mexico, although all solutions suggest \nwidespread precipitation across central and northern NM Wednesday \nand Thursday. Precipitation amounts from the NBM have trended \nhigher, with 50th percentile amounts for the 48-hour period \nstretching from Wednesday morning through Friday morning now showing \n0.5-1.5\" in most areas, with higher amounts in the northern \nmountains. 90th percentile amounts for the same period show 2-3\" \nacross almost all of central and northern NM, suggesting the \npotential for an isolated flash flood threat where heavier storms \ndevelop. New Mexico will remain in the warm sector for much of this \nstorm so snow levels will be too high for snow outside of mountain \npeaks. Another hazard to monitor with this upcoming storm system is \nthe severe threat given the strong vertical wind shear provided by \nthe incoming trough. Instability will be the limiting factor, with \nthe LREF mean showing only 200-300 J/kg of sfc based CAPE in central \nand southern areas.\n\nModel disagreement is high by the weekend, with solutions highly \ndependent on exactly how the longwave trough exits the Intermountain \nWest. That being said, drier air is likely to intrude from the west \nwith decreasing precipitation chances and cooler temperatures.",
